# Wild animals and their homes: the sea, the desert, the jungle and the ice, with it lives and it eats.
lesson language-wild-animals v=1 format=teach grade=4 unit=6 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Where animals live"
  goal "Name wild animals and the places they live in [[language.name]], and say where an animal lives and what it eats."

  look {
    scene 23x10 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        row beasts space=2 align=end {
          camel c count=1 resting=0 facing=1
          parrot p facing=-1
          frog f pose=sit facing=1
        }
      }
    }
    say """
      Every animal has its home. [[phrase.the-whale-lives-in-the-sea]], the whale lives in the sea.
      [[phrase.the-camel-lives-in-the-desert]] [[wild.the-parrot-lives-in-the-jungle]] To ask, say
      [[wild.where-does-it-live]], where does it live? To say what it eats, say [[wild.it-eats]]:
      [[phrase.the-monkey-eats-fruit]], the monkey eats fruit.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.wild-animals-name k=4
    }
    show language.wild-animals-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.wild-animals-meaning k=3 hard-k=4
    show language.wild-animals-write k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show language.wild-animals-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=6
    show language.wild-animals-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.wild-animals-write k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.wild-animals-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.wild-animals-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.wild-animals-write k=3
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show language.wild-animals-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.wild-animals-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[phrase.the-whale-lives-in-the-sea]] The whale lives in the sea. [[wild.where-does-it-live]] Where does it live? [[wild.it-eats-fruit]] It eats fruit."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is two animals of the same place taken for each other, the whale and
    the seal, the parrot and the eagle: each naming question describes the animal and offers the one
    most often mixed up. Where each animal lives is as the shelf's drawings describe it (the camel
    on sand, the seal on rocks, the parrot on an island, the frog by a pond), and the polar bear on
    the ice and the monkey in the trees are recalled. The step up from the pets of grade two is that
    an animal is now described by its home and its food, in a sentence with lives and eats. From
    this grade the written words are marked with their accents: a word typed without its accent is
    not taken. The dictation is read from this sheet, where it prints as Read aloud, or played by
    the device. If the child is stuck, sort pictures of animals into piles by where they live,
    naming each. Off the page, watch a nature programme with the sound down and name the animals and
    their homes in the language. It meets England's key stage 2 languages programme (years 3 to 6,
    statutory from age 7): "describe people, places, things and actions orally and in writing";
    Japan's grades 3 and 4 foreign language activities: "Activities to listen to simple words and
    phrases and basic expressions about their familiar people and their surroundings and match the
    phrases and expressions with illustrations and photographs"; and CEFR A1, "Can understand and
    use familiar everyday expressions and very basic phrases". The phrases are in
    [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[wild.the-seal]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[wild.the-whale]] and [[wild.the-camel]] and the words to write are [[wild.frog]] and [[wild.monkey]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[wild.the-polar-bear]] and [[wild.the-monkey]] and the words to write are [[wild.camel]], [[wild.whale]] and [[wild.parrot]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
